by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-06T14:10:13Z

Rebased on 8.2, and fixed the precedence while at it.

A route default was winning over an explicitly passed parameter: with `_query: {page: 1}` on the route, `generate("user", ["page" => 2])` returned `?page=1`. Silently wrong URL, and the opposite of what #63280 asks for in its first example. The default and the explicit `_query` were merged into the same bag, which then landed on top of the plain parameters. They are separate now:

```php
$extra = array_replace($defaultQuery, $extra, $queryParameters);
```

Also made a non-array `_query` default throw `InvalidParameterException` instead of being ignored. A non-array `_query` parameter has thrown since 8.0 (#60863), so the same key had opposite handling depending on where it came from.

Added tests for both, plus one for `null` removing an inherited key. Verified the compiled generator agrees with the plain one on all six precedence cases.

Two things left for a maintainer to weigh in on, both intentional as it stands:

- The matching side is untouched, so `$request->query` does not get these values and `#[MapQueryParameter]` does not see them. Generation only, now stated in the description.
- `_query` has been reserved as a generator parameter since 7.3, but an application using it as an ordinary route default today will now get a query string on every generated URL for that route.

